Q1. Application: Real-Time Search Index Compression
A search indexing system reduces its input size by half at each recursive step. At every level,
it performs additional indexing work proportional to the logarithm of the current input size.
Apply the substitution method to solve the recurrence T(n)=T(n/2)+log⁡n. Expand the
recurrence step-by-step and derive the final asymptotic time complexity. Analyze how the
recursion depth influences performance. Interpret the efficiency using asymptotic notation.

Given Recurrence
T(n)=T(n/2)+logn
Substitution Method (Expansion)

Expand the recurrence:

T(n)=T(n/2)+logn
=T(n/4)+log(n/2)+logn
=T(n/8)+log(n/4)+log(n/2)+logn

After k expansions,

T(n)=T(n/2k)+i=0∑k−1​log(2in​)
	​	​
The recursion stops when

2kn​=1

Therefore,

k=log2​n

Each recursive level contributes O(log n) work, and there are O(log n) levels.

Hence,

T(n)=O(logn×logn)
T(n)=O((logn)^2)	​

Recursion Depth Analysis
Input size becomes half at every recursive call.
Number of recursive levels = log₂ n.
Work at each level is proportional to log n.
Total work = log n × log n.
Asymptotic Time Complexity
O((logn)^2)​

Source Code:
import math

def search_index_compression(n):
    if n <= 1:
        return 0
    return math.log2(n) + search_index_compression(n // 2)

n = int(input("Enter the input size: "))

result = search_index_compression(n)

print("Total indexing work:", round(result, 2))
print("Asymptotic Time Complexity: O((log n)^2)")

Output:
Enter the input size: 16
Total indexing work: 10.0
Asymptotic Time Complexity: O((log n)^2)




Q2. Application: Cloud Data Backup System
A cloud backup system divides data into smaller chunks recursively:T(n) = 2T(n/2) + n log
n.Apply the Master Theorem and determine the time complexity. Identify the correct case
and justify your answer. Discuss how the algorithm scales with increasing data size and
interpret using asymptotic notation.

Given Recurrence
T(n)=2T(n/2)+nlog⁡nT(n)=2T(n/2)+n\log nT(n)=2T(n/2)+nlogn 

Applying the Master Theorem
The recurrence is of the form:
T(n)=aT(n/b)+f(n)T(n)=aT(n/b)+f(n)T(n)=aT(n/b)+f(n) 

where,
•	a=2a=2a=2 
•	b=2b=2b=2 
•	f(n)=nlog⁡nf(n)=n\log nf(n)=nlogn 

Calculate:
nlog⁡ba=nlog⁡22=nn^{\log_b a}=n^{\log_2 2}=nnlogba=nlog22=n 

Compare:
•	f(n)=nlog⁡nf(n)=n\log nf(n)=nlogn 
•	nlog⁡ba=nn^{\log_b a}=nnlogba=n 
Since
f(n)=Θ(nlog⁡1n)=Θ(nlog⁡balog⁡1n)f(n)=\Theta\left(n\log^1 n\right) =\Theta\left(n^{\log_b a}\log^1 n\right)f(n)=Θ(nlog1n)=Θ(nlogbalog1n) 
this matches Master Theorem Case 2, where
f(n)=Θ(nlog⁡balog⁡kn),k=1f(n)=\Theta\left(n^{\log_b a}\log^k n\right),\quad k=1f(n)=Θ(nlogbalogkn),k=1 
Therefore,
T(n)=Θ(nlog⁡k+1n)T(n)=\Theta\left(n\log^{k+1}n\right)T(n)=Θ(nlogk+1n) T(n)=Θ(n(log⁡n)2)\boxed{T(n)=\Theta(n(\log n)^2)}T(n)=Θ(n(logn)2) 
________________________________________

Case Identification
Master Theorem: Case 2
Because
f(n)=Θ(nlog⁡n)=Θ(nlog⁡22log⁡1n)f(n)=\Theta(n\log n)=\Theta(n^{\log_2 2}\log^1 n)f(n)=Θ(nlogn)=Θ(nlog22log1n) 
________________________________________
Scalability Analysis
•	The data is divided into two equal halves recursively. 
•	Each level performs nlog⁡nn\log nnlogn work. 
•	There are log⁡n\log nlogn levels of recursion. 
•	Hence, the total running time grows as: 
nlog⁡n×log⁡n=n(log⁡n)2n\log n \times \log n = n(\log n)^2nlogn×logn=n(logn)2 
As the data size increases, the algorithm scales efficiently but is slightly slower than O(nlog⁡n)O(n\log n)O(nlogn) because of the extra logarithmic factor.
________________________________________
Asymptotic Time Complexity
Θ(n(log⁡n)2)\boxed{\Theta(n(\log n)^2)}Θ(n(logn)2)

